<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Staffordshire Hoard</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">found <a href="/years/2009">2009</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/staffordshire">Staffordshire</a> Hoard is the largest hoard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> metalwork yet found, discovered by a metal detectorist in a field near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lichfield">Lichfield</a> in 2009. It contains several thousand pieces, mostly fittings stripped from swords and other war gear of the 7th century, many set with garnets. It is shared between museums in Birmingham and Stoke-on-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/staffordshire-hoard.jpg" alt="Staffordshire Hoard" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Rowan, Birmingham Museum and Art Gallery · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Staffordshire_hoard_annotated.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The hoard consists of almost 4,600 items and fragments, with 5.1 kg of gold, 1.4 kg of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and about 3,500 pieces of garnet cloisonné jewellery. It was most likely deposited between 650 and 675 CE, and its objects were probably made in the 6th and 7th centuries, most of them in the 7th. Apart from two or possibly three crosses, the items are military. There are no domestic objects and no women's jewellery, the more usual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> finds of gold. It includes 66 gold sword hilt collars and 86 sword pommels, the largest number of pommels ever found in one place.</p><p>A small gold strip, perhaps from a shield, a sword belt or the arm of a cross, is inscribed on both sides in insular majuscule with a biblical quotation from Numbers 10:35, rendered in the KJV as &quot;Rise up, LORD, and let thine enemies be scattered&quot;. The largest cross was folded, and explanations include that it was folded to fit a small space, by pagans with no esteem for it, or by Christians to remove its sacred character before burial.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The hoard was deposited in a remote area just south of the Roman Watling Street, within the kingdom of Mercia. Most of the gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> appears to have been deliberately removed from the objects it was attached to, so it does not look like simple loot. Brooks linked its warlike character to the custom of giving war-gear as a death duty to the king, and a parallel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beowulf">Beowulf</a> describes warriors stripping the pommels of enemy swords. Wall proposed a connection with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peada">Peada</a>, briefly king of Mercia in 655–656, and Sharp one with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lichfield">Lichfield</a> in 875.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Terry Herbert found the first gold with a metal detector on 5 July 2009 in a field near Hammerwich, and 244 gold objects were recovered in five days. The find was announced on 24 September 2009. The coroner declared it treasure, and on 25 November 2009 the Treasure Valuation Committee valued it at £3.285 million. On 23 March 2010 it was announced that the sum had been raised for the Birmingham Museum and Art Gallery and the Potteries Museum &amp; Art Gallery to buy it jointly. A further 91 items were found in the field in 2012. A research project begun that year concluded that Byzantine coins were probably a primary source of the gold and that the garnets were probably recycled from Roman objects.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Staffordshire Hoard” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Staffordshire Hoard</b>, found near Lichfield in 2009, contains several thousand pieces of gold and silver war gear from the 7th century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mercia">Kingdom of Mercia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
